/home/sat/mcrl-v025-c1c2suff-ws/EPOCH-TREND-2026-09-09.md:33:3. **Yes, FULL availability moves upward; the other arms are essentially flat.** On `P-a0`, FULL rises from 74.379% at epoch 200 to 76.440% at epoch 600 and 76.819% at epoch 2000. It is the lowest-availability arm only at epoch 200; from epoch 600 onward it slightly exceeds BASELINE, although it remains below DROP_C3. On `P-u`, FULL is still just 0.043 percentage point below BASELINE at epoch 600, then rises above it. DROP_C3 stays near 79.2%, and BASELINE stays at 76.317%. Thus the epoch-200 efficiency gain coincides with FULL serving fewer users, while the later availability recovery coincides with the EE advantage over DROP_C3 disappearing and reversing.
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-c1c2suff-ws/EPOCH-TREND-2026-09-09.md:47:- No training was run. The evaluation restored the existing epoch 200, 600, 1000, 1400, and 2000 checkpoints for 2 seeds × {FULL, DROP_C3}; BASELINE remained the fixed external policy.
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-c1c2suff-ws/MATCHED-ANCHOR-TIER-2026-09-10.md:120:- At one common prefix and lineage, eight informative/neutral combinations have at most **four C1/C2 seed families**: I/I, N/I, I/N and N/N. C3 changes ranking, not the seed. In particular, each requested ONLY arm shares a seed family with an existing DROP or all-neutral arm. Its additional catalogue work can therefore be zero **if those existing per-arm families are already computed**; this does not mean every family costs one shared catalogue. BASELINE follows its external policy.
